.TH _PRINTF 3 "August 2026" "1.0" "C Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
_printf \- custom formatted output conversion function
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include "main.h"
.PP
.BI "int _printf(const char *" format ", ...);"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B _printf()
function writes output to the standard output stream according to a format string. It mimics the core behavior of the standard C library
.BR printf (3)
function.

The format string is a null-terminated character string composed of zero or more directives: ordinary characters (not
.B %
), which are copied unchanged to the output stream, and conversion specifiers, each of which results in fetching zero or more subsequent variadic arguments.

.SH CONVERSION SPECIFIERS
A conversion specifier is introduced by the character
.B %
and terminates with a specifier character. This implementation supports the following conversion specifiers:

.TP
.B %c
The
.I int
argument is converted to an
.I unsigned char
and printed as a single character.

.TP
.B %s
The
.I "char *"
argument is expected to be a pointer to a null-terminated string. Characters are printed up to (but not including) the terminating null byte. If the pointer passed is
.B NULL
, the function safely outputs
.B (null)
.

.TP
.B %%
A literal percent sign '%' is written to standard output. No argument is converted.

.TP
.B %d, %i
The
.I int
argument is converted to signed decimal notation and printed.

.SH RETURN VALUE
Upon successful completion,
.B _printf()
returns the total number of characters printed (excluding the null byte used to terminate strings).

If an error is encountered (e.g., if the
.I format
string pointer itself is
.B NULL
or if the format string ends with an incomplete trailing
.B %
), the function returns
.B \-1
.

.SH EXAMPLES
To print a formatted string with a string specifier, an integer, and a literal percent sign:
.PP
.RS
.nf
#include "main.h"

int main(void)
{
    int len;

    len = _printf("Hello %s, score: %d%%\en", "Alice", 100);
    _printf("Printed %d characters.\en", len);

    return (0);
}
.fi
.RE

.SH BUGS AND LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 3
.B Unknown Specifiers:
Pairing
.B %
with an unsupported specifier (e.g.,
.B %z
) outputs both the percent sign and the character literally.
.IP \(bu 3
.B Trailing Percent:
A format string ending with a single
.B %
character without a specifier causes the function to return
.B \-1
.

